{
  "profiles": {
    "http": {
      "commandName": "Project",
      "environmentVariables": {
        "ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"
      },
      "dotnetRunMessages": true,
      "applicationUrl": "http://localhost:5079"
    },
    "https": {
      "commandName": "Project",
      "environmentVariables": {
        "ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"
      },
      "dotnetRunMessages": true,
      "applicationUrl": "https://localhost:7226;http://localhost:5079"
    },
    "Container (Dockerfile)": {
      "commandName": "Docker",
      "launchUrl": "{Scheme}://{ServiceHost}:{ServicePort}",
      "environmentVariables": {
        "ASPNETCORE_HTTPS_PORTS": "8081",
        "ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS": "8080"
      },
      "publishAllPorts": true,
      "useSSL": true
    }
  },
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/launchsettings.json"
}

/*
============================================================
LAUNCHSETTINGS.JSON - COMPLETE EXPLANATION
============================================================

launchSettings.json contains local development and debugging
settings for the ASP.NET Core application.

It is mainly used by Visual Studio and the .NET CLI when
running the application locally.

It defines different profiles for running the application.

In this project we have three profiles:

    1. http
    2. https
    3. Container (Dockerfile)


IMPORTANT:

launchSettings.json is mainly a DEVELOPMENT configuration file.

It is normally used for local development, debugging, and
launching the application from Visual Studio.

It is not intended to be the main production configuration
file.


============================================================
1. PROFILES
============================================================

"profiles": {

}

The profiles section contains different ways in which we can
run our ASP.NET Core application.

Each profile can have its own:

    - Command
    - Environment
    - URL
    - Port
    - Docker settings


============================================================
2. HTTP PROFILE
============================================================

"http": {
    "commandName": "Project",
    ...
}

This profile runs the ASP.NET Core application directly as a
.NET project using HTTP.


------------------------------------------------------------
commandName
------------------------------------------------------------

"commandName": "Project"

This tells Visual Studio / .NET that the application should
be launched as a normal ASP.NET Core project.

It is not running inside Docker for this profile.


------------------------------------------------------------
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

"ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"

This sets the ASP.NET Core environment to:

    Development

ASP.NET Core uses environments such as:

    Development
    Staging
    Production

When the application is running in Development mode, code
such as:

    if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
    {
        ...
    }

can execute.

In our Program.cs, Swagger is enabled in Development mode.

Therefore, when this profile is used, development-specific
features can be enabled.


------------------------------------------------------------
dotnetRunMessages
------------------------------------------------------------

"dotnetRunMessages": true

This enables additional messages when the application starts.

For example, the console can display information about the
URLs where the application is listening.


------------------------------------------------------------
applicationUrl
------------------------------------------------------------

"applicationUrl": "http://localhost:5079"

This specifies the URL where the application listens for
HTTP requests.

Our HTTP API is therefore available at:

    http://localhost:5079

For example:

    http://localhost:5079/api/employees

or:

    http://localhost:5079/api/auth/login


============================================================
3. HTTPS PROFILE
============================================================

"https": {
    "commandName": "Project",
    ...
}

This profile runs the application as a normal .NET project
but provides an HTTPS endpoint as well.


------------------------------------------------------------
commandName
------------------------------------------------------------

"commandName": "Project"

The application runs directly as an ASP.NET Core project.

Docker is not being used for this profile.


------------------------------------------------------------
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

"ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"

The application runs in the Development environment.

Therefore development-specific configuration, such as
Swagger in Program.cs, can be enabled.


------------------------------------------------------------
dotnetRunMessages
------------------------------------------------------------

"dotnetRunMessages": true

This displays additional information in the console when
the application starts.


------------------------------------------------------------
applicationUrl
------------------------------------------------------------

"applicationUrl":
    "https://localhost:7226;http://localhost:5079"

This application has two URLs in this profile:

HTTPS:

    https://localhost:7226

HTTP:

    http://localhost:5079

The semicolon separates multiple URLs.

Therefore, when you run this profile, ASP.NET Core can listen
on both endpoints.

HTTPS is generally preferred for application communication
because it encrypts data sent between the client and server.

This is especially important for authentication because
login requests contain credentials.


============================================================
4. CONTAINER / DOCKER PROFILE
============================================================

"Container (Dockerfile)": {
    "commandName": "Docker",
    ...
}

This profile is used when running the application inside a
Docker container.

Instead of running the application directly on the host
machine, the application runs inside a Docker container.


------------------------------------------------------------
commandName
------------------------------------------------------------

"commandName": "Docker"

This tells Visual Studio to use Docker for running the
application.

The Docker configuration is based on the project's
Dockerfile.


============================================================
5. LAUNCH URL
============================================================

"launchUrl": "{Scheme}://{ServiceHost}:{ServicePort}"

This specifies the URL that Visual Studio should use when
launching the application.

The values inside:

    {Scheme}
    {ServiceHost}
    {ServicePort}

are placeholders.

Visual Studio/container tooling resolves these values based
on the Docker configuration and available ports.

This allows the same launch configuration to work without
hard-coding one specific container URL.


============================================================
6. HTTPS PORT
============================================================

"ASPNETCORE_HTTPS_PORTS": "8081"

This specifies the HTTPS port used by the ASP.NET Core
application inside the container configuration.

The HTTPS port is:

    8081


============================================================
7. HTTP PORT
============================================================

"ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS": "8080"

This specifies the HTTP port used by the ASP.NET Core
application inside the container configuration.

The HTTP port is:

    8080


============================================================
8. PUBLISH ALL PORTS
============================================================

"publishAllPorts": true

This tells the container tooling to publish the container's
exposed ports.

This helps make the application's container ports accessible
from the host machine according to the Docker configuration.


============================================================
9. USE SSL
============================================================

"useSSL": true

This indicates that SSL/HTTPS should be used for the Docker
container configuration.

This works together with the HTTPS port:

    8081


============================================================
10. $schema
============================================================

"$schema":
    "https://json.schemastore.org/launchsettings.json"

The $schema property tells development tools which JSON schema
should be used to understand and validate this file.

It helps Visual Studio provide:

    - IntelliSense
    - Validation
    - Auto-completion
    - Information about supported properties

It does not represent an application runtime setting.

It is mainly useful for development tools.


============================================================
YOUR LOCAL APPLICATION URLS
============================================================

When running the normal HTTP profile:

    http://localhost:5079


When running the HTTPS profile:

    https://localhost:7226

and:

    http://localhost:5079


When running through Docker:

    HTTP  → 8080
    HTTPS → 8081


============================================================
HOW THIS CONNECTS WITH PROGRAM.CS
============================================================

The launchSettings.json file controls how the application is
started locally.

For example:

launchSettings.json
        ↓
Development environment
        ↓
Program.cs
        ↓
IsDevelopment()
        ↓
Swagger enabled


The application URLs are also used when ASP.NET Core starts:

launchSettings.json
        ↓
Application URL
        ↓
Kestrel
        ↓
ASP.NET Core Web API


============================================================
HTTP VS HTTPS
============================================================

HTTP:

    http://localhost:5079

Data is transferred without transport encryption.

HTTPS:

    https://localhost:7226

Data is encrypted using TLS/SSL.

For local development, both may be available.

For APIs involving authentication, HTTPS is important because
credentials and JWT tokens should be protected while being
transmitted.


============================================================
LAUNCHSETTINGS VS APPSETTINGS
============================================================

These two files have different responsibilities.


appsettings.json
----------------

Contains application configuration such as:

    - Database connection string
    - JWT settings
    - Logging settings
    - Application configuration


launchSettings.json
-------------------

Contains local launch/debugging configuration such as:

    - Application URLs
    - HTTP/HTTPS ports
    - Development environment
    - Docker launch settings
    - Visual Studio launch profiles


Simple difference:

    appsettings.json
        ↓
    Application configuration


    launchSettings.json
        ↓
    Local application startup / debugging configuration


============================================================
INTERVIEW-READY EXPLANATION
============================================================

"launchSettings.json is mainly used for local development and
debugging in ASP.NET Core. It defines different launch
profiles such as HTTP, HTTPS, and Docker.

In my project, the HTTP profile runs the application on
localhost:5079, while the HTTPS profile runs it on
localhost:7226 and also exposes the HTTP endpoint on 5079.

The environment variable ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT is set to
Development, which allows development-specific behavior such
as Swagger configuration in Program.cs.

The Docker profile runs the application inside a Docker
container and uses ports 8080 for HTTP and 8081 for HTTPS.

The $schema property helps Visual Studio validate the JSON and
provide IntelliSense.

Unlike appsettings.json, launchSettings.json is primarily
used for local development and application startup rather
than storing general application configuration."

============================================================
*/